  "query_preview": "..The high has shifted off to our east and the flow has \nturned more to the south to southwest. A shortwave will move across \nthe region today and we are already starting to see moisture \nincrease ahead of it early this morning. Timing of the moistures \narrival and the best dynamics do not really coincide very well so \nmost areas will see only an increase in cloud cover through this \nafternoon. However, there is still enough lift and instability for \nsome showers to develop with dry thunderstorms possible, mainly \nacross far eastern Arizona. \n\nWith the wave finally moving through today, the pressure gradient \ntightens up, resulting in stronger southwest winds around 15-25 mph \nwith gusts up to 40 mph. Some localized gusts to 45 mph are also \npossible but widespread gusts 45+ mph look marginal so no Wind \nAdvisory will issued. \n\nEven with the increase in moisture through the mid-levels, \nconditions remain pretty dry. With the dry conditions, fuels status, \nand gusty winds, we have a good set up for critical fire weather \nconditions. The Red Flag Warning remains in effect through this \nevening for much of the forecast area. Near-critical fire weather \nconditions remain likely outside of the warning area. \n\nThursday and beyond...Behind the shortwave the flow becomes more \nzonal and begins to weaken heading into Friday. Gusty southwest \nwinds will continue on Thursday but should start to lighten up some \nfor Friday. A weak trough will cross through the area on Friday but \nwith very limited moisture we won't see much more than a few \nadditional clouds during the day. A stronger wave/trough arrives \nover the weekend which will bring stronger winds on Saturday. Expect \nsouthwest to west winds 10-20 mph with gusts to 30 mph on Saturday, \nincreasing to 15-30 mph with gusts to 40 mph on Sunday. The models \nhave been stronger with this wave over the past few runs so keep an \neye on the winds as Sunday looks like it could be near Wind Advisory \nlevels in spots. Still too far out to have confidence in any \nheadlines but it is something to watch in the coming days. \nTemperatures will cool as this next feature moves into and through \nthe region. Moisture remains fleeting and most models remain dry \nthrough the weekend but depending on the depth and trajectory of the \ntrough and its parent low, we could see a few showers develop but \nconfidence remains low.",
